=== REASONING ===
1. Drop id column — not used by any Go code, and hypertables require time column in PK.
   Using DROP COLUMN (not DROP CONSTRAINT) so it works regardless of constraint name.
2. Composite PK: (vehicle_id, created_at, signal) — matches signal_observations pattern
   and ensures created_at is in the PK for hypertable partitioning.
3. chunk_time_interval: 1 day — matches signal_observations and positions patterns.
4. compress_segmentby: vehicle_id, signal — matches signal_observations pattern,
   enables per-vehicle+signal decompression for time-range queries.
5. compress_orderby: created_at DESC — standard for time-series.
6. Compression policy: 1 hour (per prompt) — aggressive but appropriate since
   signal_history is predominantly read via recent time-range queries.
7. NO retention policy — existing manual TTL in signal_history_writer.go:162 handles
   retention via user-configurable settings (retentionDays param).
8. Existing indexes (vehicle_signal_time, created) are auto-migrated to chunk-local
   indexes by create_hypertable(migrate_data => true).

